or the Adult dataset, random baseline would be around 0.5 accuracy. An 83% accuracy even with 38% noise suggests the model is still learning something meaningful. \n\nActually, wait - let me reconsider the problem. If 38% of labels are flipped:\n- The learning problem becomes much harder (this is intentional noise)\n- The model still achieves ~83% validation accuracy\n- This might actually be the expected behavior for this dataset with this noise level\n\nHowever, **0.38 seems like an unusually high noise fraction to apply by default**. Most machine learning configurations use much lower noise (like 0.05-0.10 at most) or no noise at all (0.0).\n\nLet me check if there's a suspicious pattern - the configuration has `label_noise_fraction: 0.38` which seems very high. This could be:\n1. An intentional stress test (unlikely to be \"faulty\")\n2. A configuration error where someone set it way too high by accident\n\nGiven that this is labeled a \"faulty training run\" investigation and the learning curves show poor performance, the most likely fault is **label_noise_fraction being set to an unreasonably high value (0.38)** when it should be lower or 0.0.\n\nLet me verify by checking the metrics - would we expect this validation accuracy with this noise?\n\nWith 38% of training labels randomly flipped:\n- The true signal becomes much weaker\n- A naive model that predicts the majority class might achieve ~50-62% accuracy (depending on class balance)\n- 83% accuracy with 38% noise suggests the model is still learning effectively from the corrupted data\n\nThis actually seems somewhat reasonable. Let me reconsider if there's another issue...\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the validation curve - it's quite noisy with drops (e.g., epoch 17 drops to 0.8051, epoch 15 drops to 0.8229). This is expected with high label noise in training data.\n\nLet me check if 0.38 is truly anomalous by considering what might be a more reasonable default. In typical ML practice:\n- No noise: 0.0 (most common)\n- Small noise test: 0.05-0.10\n- Medium noise: 0.15-0.20\n- High noise: 0.30+\n\n0.38 is extremely high. This appears to be a configuration fault.\n\nLet me make my diagnosis:\n\n**FAULT IDENTIFIED**: The `label_noise_fraction` is set to 0.38 (38%), which is unreasonably high. This causes severe degradation in training signal and prevents the model from achieving better validation accuracy. A reasonable value would be 0.0 (no noise) or at most 0.1 (10%) for a robustness test.\n\n**Evidence**:\n1. Configuration shows `data.label_noise_fraction: 0.38` - this is extremely high\n2. Training loss barely decreases (0.6807 to 0.6711)\n3. Validation accuracy plateaus around 0.83 with high variance, never improving\n4. The poor learning dynamics are consistent with severe label corruption",
